<?xml version="1.0" encoding="ISO-8859-1"?>
<wps:ProcessDescriptions xmlns:wps="http://www.opengis.net/wps/1.0.0" xmlns:ows="http://www.opengis.net/ows/1.1" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.opengis.net/wps/1.0.0 schemas/wps/1.0.0/wpsDescribeProcess_response.xsd" service="WPS" version="1.0.0" xml:lang="en-US">
	<ProcessDescription wps:processVersion="1" storeSupported="true" statusSupported="false">
		<ows:Identifier>MiraMon:Ombra:1</ows:Identifier>
		<ows:Title>Calculation of the limit angles that cause projected shadows in a DEM</ows:Title>
		<ows:Abstract>This program calculates, from a Digital Elevation Model (DEM) and a given solar azimuth, the Sun elevation angle from which each point in the terrain gets direct light from the Sun (it is not under the shadow of a point in that azimuth direction). This program solves also the so called "horizon problem", i.e., to determine, for a given point in a given direction (azimuth) what is the elevation angle that allows to see the sky.</ows:Abstract>
		<ows:Metadata xlink:title="Title:Calculation of the limit angles that cause projected shadows in a DEM"/>
		<ows:Metadata xlink:title="Abstract:This program calculates, from a Digital Elevation Model (DEM) and a given solar azimuth, the Sun elevation angle from which each point in the terrain gets direct light from the Sun (it is not under the shadow of a point in that azimuth direction). This program solves also the so called &amp;horizon problem&amp;, i.e., to determine, for a given point in a given direction (azimuth) what is the elevation angle that allows to see the sky."/>
		<DataInputs>
			<Input minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="1">
				<ows:Identifier>MDE</ows:Identifier>
				<ows:Title>Digital Elevation Model</ows:Title>
				<ows:Abstract>Digital Elevation Model to project the shadows Model</ows:Abstract>
				<ows:Metadata xlink:title="Param1"/>
				<ows:Metadata xlink:title="Name:DEM"/>
				<ComplexData>
					<Default>
						<Format>
							<MimeType>application/x-img</MimeType>
							<Encoding>Binary</Encoding>
						</Format>
					</Default>
					<Supported>
						<Format>
							<MimeType>application/x-img</MimeType>
							<Encoding>Binary</Encoding>
						</Format>
					</Supported>
				</ComplexData>
			</Input>
			<Input minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="1">
				<ows:Identifier>AzimutSol</ows:Identifier>
				<ows:Title>Solar Azimuth</ows:Title>
				<ows:Abstract>The solar azimuth expressed in degrees from North clockwise (eg 134.97 is approx. SE); STARS program can provide this information for any day of the year, any time, anywhere on Earth.</ows:Abstract>
				<ows:Metadata xlink:title="Param3"/>
				<ows:Metadata xlink:title="Name:SolarAzim"/>
				<LiteralData>
					<ows:DataType ows:reference="http://www.w3.org/TR/xmlschema-2/#integer">integer</ows:DataType>
					<ows:AnyValue/>
				</LiteralData>
			</Input>
			<Input minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="1">
				<ows:Identifier>MULTIBANDA</ows:Identifier>
				<ows:Title>MULTIBAND</ows:Title>
				<ows:Abstract>In case the input DEM is a multiband image, it will calculate it to all the bands.</ows:Abstract>
				<LiteralData>
					<ows:DataType ows:reference="http://www.w3.org/TR/xmlschema-2/#string">string</ows:DataType>
					<ows:AllowedValues>
						<ows:Value>##blank##</ows:Value>
					</ows:AllowedValues>
					<DefaultValue>##blank##</DefaultValue>
				</LiteralData>
			</Input>
			<Input minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="1">
				<ows:Identifier>PREFIX</ows:Identifier>
				<ows:Title>Prefix</ows:Title>
				<ows:Abstract>Specifies the prefix added to the names of source files to determine the file names for the case corrected multiband.</ows:Abstract>
				<LiteralData>
					<ows:DataType ows:reference="http://www.w3.org/TR/xmlschema-2/#string">string</ows:DataType>
					<ows:AnyValue/>
				</LiteralData>
			</Input>
		</DataInputs>
		<ProcessOutputs>
			<Output>
				<ows:Identifier>FitxerSortida</ows:Identifier>
				<ows:Title>Output File</ows:Title>
				<ows:Abstract>The resulting file is a raster (byte or byte-RLE if there are areas NODATA) whose values expressed in degrees, the minimum solar elevation angle that illuminates each point when the Sun is in the direction (azimuth ) given in the program. Therefore, the values in the output file will be in the interval [0,90). The palette is particularly suitable for OMBRA256.P25 view the file result of this program.</ows:Abstract>
				<ows:Metadata xlink:title="Param2"/>
				<ows:Metadata xlink:title="Name:OutputFile"/>
				<ComplexOutput>
					<Default>
						<Format>
							<MimeType>application/x-img</MimeType>
							<Encoding>Binary</Encoding>
						</Format>
					</Default>
					<Supported>
						<Format>
							<MimeType>application/x-img</MimeType>
							<Encoding>Binary</Encoding>
						</Format>
					</Supported>
				</ComplexOutput>
			</Output>
		</ProcessOutputs>
	</ProcessDescription>
</wps:ProcessDescriptions>